<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ne Win</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ne Win was a Burmese general and dictator who seized power in a coup in 1962 and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> for twenty-six years. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-socialist-programme-party">Burma Socialist Programme Party</a> pursued the 'Burmese Way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a>', nationalising the economy, expelling many foreigners and isolating the country, which became one of the poorest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. He resigned amid the mass protests of 1988 but kept influence for years afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ne-win.jpg" alt="Ne Win" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moshe Pridan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ne_Win_in_1959,_Jerusalem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
